1
00:00:08,131 --> 00:00:10,534
Gets harder every day lugging this body about.

2
00:00:10,573 --> 00:00:14,538
I trust you've had a fruitful day.

3
00:00:14,577 --> 00:00:18,021
I do not say happy, though I hope it has been happy.

4
00:00:18,062 --> 00:00:34,881
Namo Tassa Bhagavato Arahato Sambha Sambhodasa Namo Tassa Bhagavato Arahato Sambha Sambhodasa

5
00:00:35,603 --> 00:00:43,789
Namo tassa bhagavato arahato samma sambuddhasa.

6
00:00:44,369 --> 00:00:48,234
Homage to the Buddha, the blessed, noble and fully self-awakened one.

7
00:00:52,197 --> 00:00:58,883
So this evening we're moving on to number 29, the greater discourse on the simile of the heartwood.

8
00:01:00,043 --> 00:01:04,867
Now there's another one which is called the shorter discourse on the simile of the heartwood.

9
00:01:05,572 --> 00:01:07,716
And there are two differences, or two major ones.

10
00:01:07,757 --> 00:01:20,599
The first one seems to have been given by the Buddha on Vulture's Peak, which was in Rajgir, which is present-day Rajgir, Rajagaha, which simply means the town of the kings.

11
00:01:20,659 --> 00:01:23,885
That was where Bimbisara lived, just north of the Ganges.

12
00:01:25,671 --> 00:01:28,697
And the Buddha seems to be referring to Devadatta.

13
00:01:28,778 --> 00:01:35,852
Now Devadatta was a cousin and towards the end of his life wanted to take over the order because the Buddha was getting old, etc.

14
00:01:36,754 --> 00:01:40,320
And the Buddha refused to accept his offer as the leader of the order.

15
00:01:40,941 --> 00:01:46,313
And so very angrily, he created a schism and took some monks off with him and started a new order.

16
00:01:47,558 --> 00:01:55,828
In the meantime, of course, after his rejection by the Buddha, seemingly tried to kill the Buddha by throwing a great boulder on him and setting a wild elephant on him.

17
00:01:56,670 --> 00:01:57,370
So it's not.

18
00:01:57,990 --> 00:01:59,653
Anyway, he didn't he didn't succeed.

19
00:01:59,713 --> 00:02:06,802
And I think towards the end of his life, he's very remorseful and he and he came back.

20
00:02:07,362 --> 00:02:11,068
He's on his way to apologize to the Buddha.

21
00:02:11,587 --> 00:02:19,134
when it is said the earth opened up and swallowed him down to the Avicii hell, which is the worst of them all for creating a schism.

22
00:02:20,034 --> 00:02:21,075
That's a bit of magic there.

23
00:02:22,176 --> 00:02:30,824
The second one is given to a Brahmin called Pingala Kocha.

24
00:02:31,705 --> 00:02:35,848
So the second one is much the same, but different to another one.

25
00:02:36,288 --> 00:02:40,954
And we'll come across a signal difference in a particular phrase.

26
00:02:41,193 --> 00:02:41,493
Okay.

27
00:02:42,335 --> 00:02:44,336
So this is what this is what it says.

28
00:02:46,699 --> 00:02:59,594
Somebody going so talk about Bhikkhus now talk about monks going forth out of faith from the home life into homelessness, considering I am a victim of birth, aging and death of sorrow, lamentation, pain, grief and despair.

29
00:03:00,475 --> 00:03:03,538
I am a victim of suffering, a prey to suffering.

30
00:03:03,879 --> 00:03:07,463
Surely an ending to this whole mass of suffering can be known.

31
00:03:09,205 --> 00:03:11,228
Now, when he's gone forth, he acquires gain.

32
00:03:11,562 --> 00:03:12,984
honour and renown.

33
00:03:13,764 --> 00:03:18,409
He is pleased with that gain, honour and renown, and his intention is fulfilled.

34
00:03:19,512 --> 00:03:29,563
I have gained honour, gained honour, renown, but these other, these other monks, it is, sorry, on account of which he lauds himself and disparages others.

35
00:03:30,525 --> 00:03:38,193
I have gained honour and renown, but these other monks are unknown and of no account.

36
00:03:39,354 --> 00:04:05,447
becomes intoxicated with that gain honor and renown diligent he lives in suffering now the uh the other the other discourse says so he arises so he arouses no desire to act he makes no effort for the realization of those other states that are higher and more sublime than gain honor and renown he hangs back and slackens

37
00:04:07,552 --> 00:04:09,735
And the simile is of a tree.

38
00:04:10,515 --> 00:04:20,588
A man needing heartwood, that's right in the middle of the tree it seems, seeking heartwood, wandering in search of heartwood, came to a great tree standing possessed of heartwood.

39
00:04:21,329 --> 00:04:31,680
Passing over the heartwood, its sapling wood, its inner bark and its outer bark, he would cut off the twigs and leaves and take them away thinking they were heartwood.

40
00:04:32,521 --> 00:04:35,264
Then a man with good sight seeing him might say,

41
00:04:35,548 --> 00:05:03,091
this good man did not know that heartwood the sapwood the inner bark the outward all the twigs and leaves thus while needing heartwood seeking heartwood wandering in search of heartwood he came to a great tree standing possessed of heartwood and passing over its heartwood its sapwood its inner bark and its outer bark he cut off the twigs and leaves and took them away thinking they were heartwood

42
00:05:04,625 --> 00:05:11,192
Whatever the good man had to make with heartwood, his purpose will not be served.

43
00:05:11,211 --> 00:05:26,107
So too, such a bhikkhu who only joins the order for gain, honor and renown is like one who takes twigs and leaves of the holy life and stops short with that.

44
00:05:26,728 --> 00:05:32,673
So those two passages are now repeated with the core changes, okay?

45
00:05:32,975 --> 00:06:02,187
so here is somebody who's gone forth and we can also mean meditators meditators who go forth in order to be proud of being uh meditators who meditate in order to be proud of meditation go around saying well you know i practice vipassana meditation now in the west of course that doesn't that doesn't carry very far but in the east it might be a real plus to your job application if you say to somebody you know well i i go to the monastery every

46
00:06:02,523 --> 00:06:05,586
Every week I meditate in the morning and afternoon.

47
00:06:05,925 --> 00:06:08,127
So it counts for something in Buddhist countries.

48
00:06:08,908 --> 00:06:15,935
It presumes that you're more mindful, more wise, more in control of yourself, etc.

49
00:06:16,314 --> 00:06:18,937
But in the West, of course, it counts for virtually nothing.

50
00:06:19,778 --> 00:06:27,545
So none of us, I don't think any of us Westerners will be meditating for gain, honor and renown.

51
00:06:28,084 --> 00:06:29,406
A monk, of course, might do it.

52
00:06:29,526 --> 00:06:32,209
A bhikkhu might do it or a bhikkhunian, a nun.

53
00:06:32,492 --> 00:06:34,995
in order to be well known, so they get food.

54
00:06:37,178 --> 00:06:39,180
It's a bit cynical.

55
00:06:39,201 --> 00:06:41,543
Okay, so now we have the same thing.

56
00:06:41,584 --> 00:06:54,279
So he does not become, so the next type of monastic is that he does not become intoxicated with gain, honor, and renown.

57
00:06:54,459 --> 00:06:57,182
He does not grow negligent and fall into negligence.

58
00:06:57,843 --> 00:07:01,267
Being diligent, he achieves the attainment of virtue.

59
00:07:02,935 --> 00:07:07,341
He is pleased with the attainment of virtue and his intention is fulfilled.

60
00:07:08,324 --> 00:07:11,569
On account of it, he lords himself and disparages others.

61
00:07:12,350 --> 00:07:17,358
I am virtuous of good character, but these other monks are immoral of evil character.

62
00:07:18,079 --> 00:07:26,833
He becomes intoxicated with that attainment of virtue, grows negligent, falls into negligence, and being negligent, lives in suffering.

63
00:07:27,656 --> 00:07:29,940
He arouses no desire to act.

64
00:07:29,980 --> 00:07:40,660
He makes no effort for the realization of those other states that are higher and more sublime than virtue, and he hangs back and slackens.

65
00:07:40,680 --> 00:07:47,892
So this is one who, living the holy life or living according to the precepts,

66
00:07:48,142 --> 00:08:05,476
uh you know thinks of themselves as being better than others so that's a bit of a conceit there and uh you might think well you know that's enough for this life but in which case you've lost out on the game often in the order you'll find uh

67
00:08:06,081 --> 00:08:10,267
monks and nuns were very proud of keeping the Vinaya, the rule, very strictly.

68
00:08:10,767 --> 00:08:13,853
So it can be a bit of a falling ground.

69
00:08:14,432 --> 00:08:25,088
And there's also this terrible dilemma because you can't be fully liberated until you are purified, but you can't be purified until you're fully liberated.

70
00:08:25,629 --> 00:08:30,875
So that's a real sort of conundrum and it's best not to even think about it.

71
00:08:33,820 --> 00:08:35,261
So now,

72
00:08:36,389 --> 00:08:45,341
So it's just like somebody who is seeking heartwood and instead goes away with the outer bark, thinking the outer bark is the actual heartwood.

73
00:08:45,682 --> 00:08:54,994
And in the same way, he would be disappointed when he tries to make what he wants to make out of that bark instead of heartwood.

74
00:08:57,677 --> 00:09:04,287
So now, being pleased with the attainment of virtue, so this is the third type of monastic,

75
00:09:05,043 --> 00:09:06,765
But his intention is not fulfilled.

76
00:09:06,865 --> 00:09:10,230
He does not on account of it lord himself and disparage others.

77
00:09:11,052 --> 00:09:14,417
He does not become intoxicated with that attainment of virtue.

78
00:09:14,778 --> 00:09:19,364
He does not grow negligent and fall into negligence.

79
00:09:19,384 --> 00:09:22,528
Being diligent, he achieves the attainment of concentration.

80
00:09:23,250 --> 00:09:27,636
He's pleased with that attainment of concentration and his intention is fulfilled.

81
00:09:28,357 --> 00:09:29,438
I am concentrated.

82
00:09:29,600 --> 00:09:31,201
My mind is unified.

83
00:09:31,604 --> 00:09:35,671
But these other monks are unconcentrated and their minds astray.

84
00:09:36,533 --> 00:09:44,908
He becomes intoxicated with that attainment of concentration, grows negligent, falls into negligence and being negligent, he lives in suffering.

85
00:09:45,609 --> 00:09:47,893
So he arouses no desire to act.

86
00:09:47,913 --> 00:09:54,625
He makes no effort for the realization of those other states that are higher and more sublime than

87
00:09:54,993 --> 00:10:15,937
than uh than concentration so here concentration is uh referring mainly to the absorption states so it's also for us meditators ordinary meditators where all we're trying to do is get concentrated in our practice and of course when you are concentrated you can feel very very beautiful

88
00:10:16,389 --> 00:10:26,549
You can lift your heart to a certain level of joy or just peacefulness and pleasure and think, well, you know, this is enough for the time being.

89
00:10:27,171 --> 00:10:29,816
But in which case we miss on the real goal.

90
00:10:31,339 --> 00:10:34,365
So it's like somebody who takes the inner bark.

91
00:10:34,480 --> 00:10:48,138
So there seems to be in trees, I don't know much about trees, a bark, and then there's an inner bark, and somebody who takes the inner bark thinking to himself that, oh, this is the heartwood.

92
00:10:48,158 --> 00:10:49,899
And so, of course, he would be disappointed.

93
00:10:50,321 --> 00:10:52,484
His purpose will not be served.

94
00:10:55,628 --> 00:10:59,432
And it's just like somebody who, yeah, will not be served.

95
00:10:59,773 --> 00:11:01,095
The next one is,

96
00:11:02,695 --> 00:11:06,139
Somebody who is pleased with concentration.

97
00:11:06,159 --> 00:11:08,923
So he's got his gain, honor, and renown.

98
00:11:09,323 --> 00:11:10,144
He's got his virtue.

99
00:11:10,164 --> 00:11:12,427
He's got his concentration, but he's not happy with that.

100
00:11:12,749 --> 00:11:18,355
He's now going for, he's not intoxicated with the attainment of concentration.

101
00:11:18,416 --> 00:11:20,799
He does not grow negligent, fall into negligence.

102
00:11:21,240 --> 00:11:23,943
And being diligent, he achieved knowledge and vision.

103
00:11:24,227 --> 00:11:26,652
Now, this is not referring to insight.

104
00:11:26,672 --> 00:11:31,259
It's referring to the higher powers, it seems, according to the commentaries.

105
00:11:31,740 --> 00:11:39,511
So things like divine eye, where you can see beyond what the physical eye can see, or you can hear what is beyond.

106
00:11:40,474 --> 00:11:45,421
Yeah, all those magical things that we don't believe.

107
00:11:45,981 --> 00:11:48,647
But here I have a little story for you.

108
00:11:48,966 --> 00:11:52,091
Some of you have heard this before, I'm sure.

109
00:11:52,628 --> 00:12:03,164
But once when I was teaching in the Isle of Man, I met a Jungian therapist and he used to give talks in the evening and we used to go along and listen.

110
00:12:03,566 --> 00:12:04,167
It's very good.

111
00:12:04,807 --> 00:12:10,277
And he told me while we were speaking together, he told me that he'd been to Burma.

112
00:12:10,857 --> 00:12:12,941
He'd become very interested in the Dharma.

113
00:12:13,081 --> 00:12:16,527
He'd gone to Rangoon to the main monasteries there.

114
00:12:16,826 --> 00:12:24,077
told him he wanted to learn about Buddhism, and they sent him up to Mandalay, where the university monasteries are.

115
00:12:24,919 --> 00:12:27,504
And he took with him, of course, a translator.

116
00:12:28,104 --> 00:12:39,201
He said when he was introduced to a very elderly monk who sat in this room that was just full of books, and he just sat on a chair in the midst of all these books, it seems.

117
00:12:40,023 --> 00:12:41,885
And they had a great conversation.

118
00:12:42,125 --> 00:12:45,230
I remember him saying to me, it lasted about two hours.

119
00:12:45,919 --> 00:12:51,666
And it was only as he was leaving the monastery that he realized he hadn't been using the translator.

120
00:12:52,687 --> 00:12:58,652
And that look of sort of, you know, disbelief was still on his face when he told me.

121
00:12:59,373 --> 00:13:01,215
So how can you explain that?

122
00:13:03,136 --> 00:13:05,399
So these are the higher powers.

123
00:13:07,120 --> 00:13:11,725
Anyway, this man also falls into negligence.

124
00:13:12,058 --> 00:13:39,990
makes no desire to act no effort for the realization of those other states that are higher and more sublime than uh than these powers you get lost in intoxicated by these powers and that's the person who takes away the sapwood so that's the inner part he's near he's near the heartwood but he's mistaken the sapwood for the heartwood and whatever whatever it was this good man had to make with heartwood

125
00:13:40,206 --> 00:13:45,092
His purpose will not be served.

126
00:13:45,113 --> 00:13:47,455
So then we come to the final position.

127
00:13:47,796 --> 00:13:52,620
So somebody has attained all these things, but he's not pleased.

128
00:13:52,662 --> 00:13:56,125
He's not pleased with the achievement of knowledge and vision.

129
00:13:57,047 --> 00:14:00,750
And he does not on that account, laud himself and disparage others.

130
00:14:01,351 --> 00:14:04,335
He does not become intoxicated with that knowledge and vision.

131
00:14:04,575 --> 00:14:08,559
He does not grow negligent and fall into negligence.

132
00:14:08,876 --> 00:14:37,871
being diligent he attains perpetual liberation and it is impossible for that bhikkhu to fall away from perpetual deliberation uh deliverance excuse me so here finally somebody uh has achieved the full purpose of our path which is to become liberated from suffering so it's as though uh a person went to a tree cutting off only its heartwood

133
00:14:37,970 --> 00:14:40,432
He took it away knowing it was hard work.

134
00:14:40,734 --> 00:14:43,697
And whatever it was, this good man had to make with heartwood.

135
00:14:44,019 --> 00:14:46,322
His purpose was well served.

136
00:14:47,683 --> 00:14:51,308
So and then the chapter, the final pieces.

137
00:14:51,649 --> 00:15:06,389
So this holy life and he's not only now remember, because anybody who's listening, anybody who's meditating is not for gain, honor, renown for its benefit or the attainment of virtue for its benefit.

138
00:15:06,639 --> 00:15:21,543
Now, remember, virtue here also means all this stuff of purifying the heart, everything that we would normally think of as well as psychotherapy, but also, you know, moral virtue, ethical virtues.

139
00:15:23,505 --> 00:15:32,580
It's not for the attainment of concentration purely, and these in the higher form are those absorptions, nor is it for the attainment of these powers.

140
00:15:33,302 --> 00:15:41,253
But it is this unshakable deliverance of the heart that is the goal of the holy life and its heart would end its end.

141
00:15:42,554 --> 00:15:49,063
OK, so when we sit to meditate, the purification is going on.

142
00:15:49,563 --> 00:15:51,226
We don't have to worry about it at all.

143
00:15:53,870 --> 00:15:59,918
But it is to bring the mind to a somewhat level of concentration, a steadiness of attention.

144
00:15:59,981 --> 00:16:08,235
And our purpose is, of course, to see the three characteristics of existence, which will bring about liberation from all suffering.

145
00:16:08,254 --> 00:16:17,350
And the end of the discourse, which was given to the Brahmin Pingala Kocha,

146
00:16:17,768 --> 00:16:21,672
Of course, he's quite taken by this wonderful discourse by the Buddha.

147
00:16:22,253 --> 00:16:25,196
And as usual, we have this pion.

148
00:16:26,017 --> 00:16:29,441
Magnificent Master Gautama, magnificent Master Gautama.

149
00:16:29,780 --> 00:16:36,229
Master Gautama has made the Dharma clear in many ways, as though he were turning upright but had been overthrown.

150
00:16:37,850 --> 00:16:44,518
Revealing what was hidden, showing the way to one who was lost, holding up a lamp in the dark to those who can see,

151
00:16:45,038 --> 00:16:49,445
I go to Master Gautama for refuge to the Dharma and the Sangha of Bhikkhu.

152
00:16:50,246 --> 00:16:57,158
From today, let Master Gautama remember me as a lay follower who has gone to him for refuge for life.

153
00:16:57,739 --> 00:17:02,386
So he got another follower there with that talk.

154
00:17:02,407 --> 00:17:06,574
So a rather interesting little talk just to keep us on the straight and narrow.

155
00:17:08,478 --> 00:17:17,773
So I do hope that that is inspiring and that you will continue giving all your best to the practice of liberation.

156
00:17:18,734 --> 00:17:23,222
May you be liberated from all suffering sooner rather than later.

157
00:17:24,885 --> 00:17:26,689
So we can sit for a while.

